@startuml flow-direct-message
' Direct messages: who may message whom, and where each check happens.
'
' Render:  plantuml docs/diagrams/flow-direct-message.puml

' Explicit near-white, NOT transparent: the text is dark, and a transparent background
' renders black on a dark viewer, which makes the whole diagram unreadable.
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #FDFDFD
  BorderColor #5B5BD6
  ArrowColor #59606F
  DiamondBackgroundColor #EFF1F6
  DiamondBorderColor #5B5BD6
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFF8E1
  BorderColor #C2670A
}

title Sending a direct message

start

:POST /api/messages/conversations/<id>
with a message body;

note right
  The sender is the **session's** user. No endpoint
  in this feature takes a user id from a body or a
  path, so there is no request shape that could ask
  to write as somebody else.
end note

if (conversation exists AND the sender is in it?) then (no)
  #FFE0E0:**404** No such conversation;
  note right
    404, not 403. A 403 would confirm the id exists,
    which lets anyone count how many conversations
    the application holds by walking ids.
  end note
  stop
endif

if (do they still follow **each other**?) then (no)
  #FFE0E0:**403** You can only message peers
  who follow you back;
  note right
    Re-checked on **every send**, not only when the
    conversation was opened. Unfollowing is how
    somebody stops unwanted contact, so it has to
    take effect immediately -- checked once at open,
    a conversation would be a permanent channel that
    no later action could close.

    History stays readable. Deleting it would destroy
    both people's copy of the exchange; refusing new
    messages stops the contact, which is the part
    that matters.
  end note
  stop
endif

if (sender over 20 messages/minute?) then (yes)
  #FFE0E0:**429** Slow down a moment;
  note right: Per sender, across every\nconversation. Generous for a\nperson, ruinous for a script.
  stop
endif

#E8F5E9:save the message
stamp last_message_at
mark read for the sender;

:publish to the **recipient's** open streams;
:publish to the **sender's** other streams;

note right
  Both sides. The recipient sees it arrive; the
  sender's other tabs stay in step, and the sending
  tab appends the message when it comes back --
  so a send and a receive take the same path and
  cannot render differently.

  Delivery is best-effort by design: the message is
  already committed, so a dead stream must never
  fail the send. The client picks it up on its next
  fetch either way.
end note

:**200** with the saved message;

stop

' A diagram-level footnote. `note bottom` cannot follow `stop` -- after the flow ends there is
' no activity for a note to attach to, which is a syntax error rather than a floating note.
legend bottom
  **Opening** a conversation runs the same mutual-follow check
  before it will create one, and the pair is stored in canonical
  order (lo id, hi id) under a UNIQUE -- so two people opening a
  thread simultaneously get the same row rather than one each.
endlegend

@enduml
